    <statement><p>Find the eigenvalues of the matrix <m>A</m>.
<me>A = \left[\begin{array}{cccc}
-1 \amp 6 \amp 0 \amp 0\cr
0 \amp -7 \amp 0 \amp 0\cr
0 \amp 0 \amp -4 \amp 4\cr
0 \amp 0 \amp -24 \amp 16
\end{array}\right].</me></p>
<p>The eigenvalues are <m>\lambda_1 \lt  \lambda_2 \lt  \lambda_3 \lt  \lambda_4</m>, where</p>
<p><m>\lambda_1 =</m> <fillin characters="5" name="AnSwEr0001"/>
has an eigenvector <fillin rows="4"/>,
<m>\lambda_2 =</m> <fillin characters="5" name="AnSwEr0003"/>
has an eigenvector <fillin rows="4"/>,</p>
<p><m>\lambda_3 =</m> <fillin characters="5" name="AnSwEr0005"/>
has an eigenvector <fillin rows="4"/>,
<m>\lambda_4 =</m> <fillin characters="5" name="AnSwEr0007"/>
has an eigenvector <fillin rows="4"/>.</p>
<p><alert>Note:</alert> you may want to use a graphing calculator to estimate the roots of the polynomial which defines
the eigenvalues.</p>
</statement>
